content.theory:
CSS **custom properties** — commonly called CSS variables — let you store a value once and reuse it everywhere. Change one line and the entire design updates in place. Define them with `--name` and read them with `var()`:

```css
:root {
  --main-color: #6366f1;
  --spacing: 16px;
}
.card {
  background: var(--main-color);
  padding: var(--spacing);
}
```
`:root` is the highest scope (the document root element), so variables defined there cascade to every element on the page.

### var() and fallbacks
```css
.badge {
  background: var(--brand-color, #e11d48);
}
```
The second argument to `var()` is a **fallback**, used when the variable is undefined or invalid. Fallbacks are essential for progressive enhancement and for shared components whose parent may or may not define a variable.

### Scoped overrides
Custom properties cascade like any other declaration, so you can redefine a variable in a narrower scope and everything below it picks up the new value:

```css
.card { --accent: #2563eb; }
.card.danger { --accent: #dc2626; }
```
A `.card.danger` renders red, a plain `.card` renders blue — one variable, two themes, zero duplicated rules.

### calc() with variables
```css
.card {
  padding: calc(var(--spacing) * 2);
  width: calc(100% - var(--spacing));
}
```
`calc()` lets you do arithmetic on sizes and colors — and it composes with variables, so derived values update automatically whenever the variable changes. That is how design systems derive spacing scales from a single base value.

### Why theming is easier
Dark mode is the classic win. Define every color as a variable, then override the whole set under a scope:

```css
:root { --bg: #ffffff; --text: #111827; }
[data-theme='dark'] { --bg: #0f172a; --text: #f8fafc; }
body { background: var(--bg); color: var(--text); }
```
Flip the `data-theme` attribute on `<html>` and the entire app re-themes instantly — no per-component edits, no `!important` overrides.

Common mistakes: defining a variable inside an element that only covers part of the tree (custom properties inherit *downward*), relying on a variable with no fallback when it might be undefined, and trying to use them where older browsers demand a literal value (like keyframe animation names). Use variables for colors, spacing, radii, and typography — not for values you never intend to change.
